{"title":"The Epic v. Google trial may come down to simple v. complicated (The Verge, 2023-11-07T15:00:40+00:00)","text":"The Epic v. Google trial may come down to simple v. complicated (The Verge, 2023-11-07T15:00:40+00:00)
The upshot for consumers, Epic’s earlier legal filings have suggested, is that we pay higher prices for apps than we would if there were more competition and / or lower app store and payment processing fees. But while this will probably come up later in the trial, Epic chose to focus more on simply painting Google as the bad guy on day one.

It’s not clear how much of that evidence will hold up on closer examination. That $360 million, for instance, refers to an alleged payment that kept Activision from opening an app store that could compete with Google Play. But Activision told The Verge in 2022 that it “never entered into an agreement that Activision would not open its own app store” — and Google is now, it says, armed with the evidence to prove it. On Monday, Epic’s attorney admitted Google “was too clever” to draw up contracts that specifically forced developers not to compete with the Play Store. The overall narrative is compelling, though — and I’m not sure Google’s opening statement countered it.","entities":["Google Play","Activision","Epic","Google","The Epic v. Google trial may come down to simple v. complicated","app store and payment processing fees","Epic v. Google trial","competition","The Verge"],"facts":["More from the US v Google trial: vertical search, pre-installs and the case of Firefox/Yahoo -MENTIONS-> Google Play","ChatGPT: Everything you need to know about the AI-powered chatbot -MENTIONS-> Google Play","Tim Sweeney on Epic’s victory royale over Google -MENTIONS-> Google Play","What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today -MENTIONS-> Google Play","Beeper Mini is back in operation after Apple’s attempt to shut it down -MENTIONS-> Google Play"]}

{"title":"What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today (TechCrunch, 2023-11-06T16:47:11+00:00)","text":"What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today (TechCrunch, 2023-11-06T16:47:11+00:00)
This program, still in pilot testing, is open to all developers who sell apps in the 35 markets where it’s now available, including the U.S., and reduces the standard commission by 4% for companies who choose to use their own payment processing solution. Spotify and Bumble were the initial testers for the new system, first introduced in November 2022, and Match, as part of its settlement agreement, will also now take advantage of this option.

Google will also argue that its commissions aren’t just tied to payment processing, but rather help it to develop other features and controls to keep Android users safe and guide them to discovering new applications, as well as help fund the tools and services developers need to build and grow their apps. Google also helps developers reach a broader audience beyond the mobile phone, with support for Android across platforms, ranging from tablets to TVs to autos and more. And it will note that 99% of its developers qualify for a commission of 15% or less, based on their revenues.","entities":["Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google","Android","What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today","Epic Games","Match","TechCrunch","Bumble","Google","Spotify","U.S.","Fortnite"],"facts":["ChatGPT: Everything you need to know about the AI-powered chatbot -MENTIONS-> Android","The best Black Friday deals you can get for under $25 -MENTIONS-> Android","More from the US v Google trial: vertical search, pre-installs and the case of Firefox/Yahoo -MENTIONS-> Android","ChatGPT’s mobile app hits record $4.58M in revenue in September -MENTIONS-> Android","European football: Eintracht Frankfurt hit Bayern for five in famous thrashing -MENTIONS-> Android"]}

