“The Sphere does a similar thing, [in that] you have a fully immersive space, the video screen in the Sphere is vast, it has a huge number of pixels, and you can immerse the audience within this environment.” So what does all this mean for the concert industry, which has historically built its lucrative concert spin-off industry on the conversion of sports venues to rock concert stages? It certainly means the future is looking a little more ... spherical? And, from a technological perspective, a little more plug-and-play. “It opens you up to thinking about performances in different ways, both from a creative point of view, from a business point of view, from a sustainability point of view, and the ability to not have to bring your own structure and screen and [sound system],” Lipson says. ABBA’s simulacrums on stage at ABBA Arena in London.","entities":["The Sydney Morning Herald","London","ABBA","concert industry","ABBA Arena","The Sphere","Lipson","sports venues","rock concert stages","Inside the $US2.3b venue that will transform the concert industry"],"facts":["ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to open lower as Wall Street closes September with more losses -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","The best shows to stream in December -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","McRae reveals hidden message he carried under collar of his grand final shirt -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to drop as Wall Street’s September slump deepens -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald"]}
